  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1126 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nSkies will remain cloudy this afternoon as a deepening low pressure \nsystem approaches from the west coast. Strengthening warm air \nadvection out of the south, ahead of the system, will help produce \nspotty showers and areas of drizzle later this afternoon and \nevening. Highs today will top out in the mid to upper 60s under the \ndreary conditions. Wide spread rain chances will continue to \noverspread eastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa overnight, as low \ntemperatures dip into the mid to upper 50s.\n\nSoutherly winds will increase ahead of the approaching low Monday \nmorning, reaching speeds up to 35 to 45 mph. The push of warmer air \nout of the south will draw highs back into the upper 60s and low \n70s, Monday, as showers and a few rumbles of thunder continue to \nmove through.   \n\nStorm chances increase Monday evening, as up to 500-1000 J/kg of \ninstability pools ahead of an approaching cold front. Moderate low \nlevel turning evident in forecast hodographs, as well as 40-50 kts \nof deep layer shear provide support for the development of strong to \nsevere storms across far southeast Nebraska Monday evening. These \nstorms could be capable of producing damaging wind gusts and perhaps \na tornado or two, given the low cloud bases and tightly wound Low \npressure system. However, weak lapse rates and persistent dreary \ncloud cover could limit storm potential. For now, the SPC maintains \na Marginal (level 1 of 5) Risk for severe storms southeast of a line \nfrom Albion to Wayne, NE.\n\nMonday Night through Wednesday...\n\nThe dry slot of the system passes over the region behind line of \npotential storms Monday night, giving most locations a brief \nreprieve from precipitation.\n\nHowever, this will be followed by the deformation zone, during the \nafternoon Tuesday, bringing another round of rain. Winds ramp up as \nthe backside of the low moves through, with northerly gusts up to 25 \nto 35 mph. Afternoon highs will be notably cooler, ranging from \naround 50 in far northeast Nebraska to the low 60s in southeast \nNebraska/southwest Iowa.\n\nShowers exit the area to the northeast early Wednesday, with cooler \ntemperatures lingering. Lows dropping into the mid 30s to low 40s \nTuesday night will only rise into the 50s for most locations \nWednesday. \n\nThursday and Beyond...\n\nA second, weaker shortwave looks to move out of the Four Corners \nregion through the Southern Plains mid to late week. This could \nbring a line of showers across far southeast Nebraska Thursday, \nhowever there remains quite a bit of disparities in timing and \nlocation of the wave with the deterministic models.  \n\nOverall, high temperatures are expected to remain in the 50s to low \n60s, with overnight lows falling into the 30s and 40s through the \nend of the coming week. Any chances for rain beyond the \nMonday/Tuesday system remain low at < 20%.",
